{
  "manufacturer": "Contemporary Controls",
  "manufacturer_slug": "contemporary-controls",
  "model": "Fireye PPC4000 parallel positioning control via BASgatewayLX",
  "short_model": "BASgatewayLX Fireye PPC4000",
  "model_slug": "basgatewaylx-fireye-ppc4000",
  "aliases": [
    "PPC4000",
    "PPC 4000",
    "Fireye PPC-4000"
  ],
  "equipment_type": "Boiler burner management (parallel positioning)",
  "applies_to": "Fireye PPC4000 fuel/air parallel positioning controls on boilers, read over Modbus by a BASgatewayLX.",
  "summary": "Gateway profile for the Fireye PPC4000: 153 Analog Inputs, 43 polled by default. It reads operational state, burner hours and cycles, modulation rate and mode, servo and VFD positions, O2 trim values, sensor readings, the lockout history and the burner control flags.",
  "bacnet_transport": [
    "BACnet IP"
  ],
  "firmware": null,
  "default_device_instance": null,
  "access_inferred": true,
  "notes": [
    "Built from Contemporary Controls' BASgatewayLX Modbus device profile Fireye_PPC4000_a_LX.csv; the profile maps the registers in the PPC4000 Modbus register list (https://www.ccontrols.com/support/dp/MOD-4001.pdf).",
    "Object instances are the ones this profile assigns when it is loaded into the gateway. A site that edited or rebuilt the profile may use different instances.",
    "Only 43 of the 153 rows are flagged for polling in the profile; the rest are served only after you tick them in the gateway. Their descriptions say \"not polled by default\".",
    "The CSV has no read/write column: Analog/Binary Inputs are read-only and Analog/Binary Outputs are commandable (priority array), which is how the gateway maps them (access inferred). Descriptions give the Modbus register each object reads.",
    "Some names appear more than once in the profile as published (Lockout History - Fault 1 - Date of Fault Occurrence; Lockout History - Fault 2 - Date of Fault Occurrence; Lockout History - Fault 3 - Date of Fault Occurrence...); they are kept as printed and told apart by instance."
  ],
  "source": {
    "title": "BASgatewayLX Modbus device profile Fireye_PPC4000_a",
    "doc_number": "Fireye_PPC4000_a_LX.csv",
    "revision_date": null,
    "url": "https://www.ccontrols.com/support/dp/FireyePPC4000.zip"
  },
  "integration": {
    "summary": "Read operational state, modulation rate and the burner control flags; enable the lockout history if you want fault codes. The profile has no writable objects.",
    "minimum_points": [
      {
        "ref": "AI 1",
        "name": "Operational State",
        "why": "Operational state"
      },
      {
        "ref": "AI 149",
        "name": "Burner Control On/Off",
        "why": "Burner on/off control state"
      },
      {
        "ref": "AI 5",
        "name": "Current Modulation Rate",
        "why": "Current modulation rate"
      },
      {
        "ref": "AI 6",
        "name": "Modulation Mode",
        "why": "Modulation mode"
      },
      {
        "ref": "AI 152",
        "name": "Burner Control AutoManual",
        "why": "Auto/manual state"
      },
      {
        "ref": "AI 3",
        "name": "Burner on minutes",
        "why": "Burner on minutes"
      },
      {
        "ref": "AI 42",
        "name": "Lockout History – Current active error number",
        "why": "Current active error number"
      }
    ],
    "enable": null,
    "heartbeat": null,
    "remote_sensors": null,
    "setup": [
      "The BASgatewayLX presents each Modbus device as its own virtual BACnet device on BACnet IP; give it a unique device instance and Modbus address (serial) or IP address (Modbus TCP) when you add it with this profile.",
      "One BASgatewayLX handles up to 100 Modbus devices and 1000 polled points in total, so leave unneeded rows unpolled."
    ],
    "gotchas": [
      "The Burner Control objects (AI 149-153) are inputs in this profile, so they show state only.",
      "Unpolled rows do not appear as BACnet objects until enabled in the gateway's device profile page."
    ]
  },
